WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.405 --> 00:00:02.280
YUFENG GUO: What happens
when our training is

00:00:02.280 --> 00:00:03.930
too big to fit on our machine?

00:00:03.930 --> 00:00:06.510
Or training in the model
starts to take hours?

00:00:06.510 --> 00:00:08.504
We go to the Cloud, of course.

00:00:08.504 --> 00:00:10.920
When you have a lot of data
such that you can't reasonably

00:00:10.920 --> 00:00:12.940
run the training on
your local machine,

00:00:12.940 --> 00:00:15.640
or the size of that data is
larger than your hard drive,

00:00:15.640 --> 00:00:17.550
it's time to look
at other options.

00:00:17.550 --> 00:00:20.040
One solid option is to shift
the machine learning training

00:00:20.040 --> 00:00:22.860
to another computer with
access to more storage,

00:00:22.860 --> 00:00:24.570
thus freeing up your
hard drive space,

00:00:24.570 --> 00:00:26.236
and allowing you to
work on other things

00:00:26.236 --> 00:00:28.844
while that training
is taking place.

00:00:28.844 --> 00:00:30.510
Let's break down
exactly what parts need

00:00:30.510 --> 00:00:31.830
to be moved into the Cloud.

00:00:31.830 --> 00:00:33.510
It's useful to think
about our training

00:00:33.510 --> 00:00:36.330
as needing two primary
resources-- compute

00:00:36.330 --> 00:00:37.590
and storage.

00:00:37.590 --> 00:00:39.300
What's interesting
here is that we

00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:41.610
don't have to tie them together
quite as tightly as you

00:00:41.610 --> 00:00:42.990
might at first expect.

00:00:42.990 --> 00:00:44.940
We can decouple
them, which yields

00:00:44.940 --> 00:00:46.950
specialized systems for both.

00:00:46.950 --> 00:00:49.170
And this can lead to
efficiencies of scale

00:00:49.170 --> 00:00:51.540
when you're dealing
with big data.

00:00:51.540 --> 00:00:54.600
Now, compute load is moved
around easily enough.

00:00:54.600 --> 00:00:56.460
But the moving of
large data sets,

00:00:56.460 --> 00:00:58.390
that can be a bit more involved.

00:00:58.390 --> 00:01:01.260
However, if your data
set is truly large,

00:01:01.260 --> 00:01:03.210
the results are
worthwhile, as it

00:01:03.210 --> 00:01:05.850
allows the data to be
accessed by many machines

00:01:05.850 --> 00:01:08.130
in parallel that are
working on your machine

00:01:08.130 --> 00:01:10.230
learning training job.

00:01:10.230 --> 00:01:12.900
Google Cloud platform
has a couple of easy ways

00:01:12.900 --> 00:01:14.970
to tie together
these abstractions.

00:01:14.970 --> 00:01:17.490
First, we'll want to make sure
that our data is on Google

00:01:17.490 --> 00:01:20.250
Cloud Storage, or GCS.

00:01:20.250 --> 00:01:22.230
We can do this using
a variety of tools.

00:01:22.230 --> 00:01:25.860
For the smaller to medium
data sets, just use gsutil.

00:01:25.860 --> 00:01:27.780
It's a command line tool
that was specifically

00:01:27.780 --> 00:01:30.960
made for interacting with
Google Cloud Storage.

00:01:30.960 --> 00:01:33.810
And it supports a -m
option that allows

00:01:33.810 --> 00:01:37.230
for sending multiple streams
in parallel, thus speeding up

00:01:37.230 --> 00:01:38.800
your transfer job.

00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:41.670
But if your data is too big
to send over the network,

00:01:41.670 --> 00:01:45.030
you can use the Google transfer
appliance, which is literally

00:01:45.030 --> 00:01:47.490
a machine that will be
shipped to your data center

00:01:47.490 --> 00:01:51.690
to securely capture and transfer
a whole petabyte of data.

00:01:51.690 --> 00:01:53.770
With a typical network
bandwidth of, say,

00:01:53.770 --> 00:01:57.450
100 megabits per second,
it would take three years

00:01:57.450 --> 00:02:00.180
to upload a petabyte of
data over the network.

00:02:00.180 --> 00:02:02.280
Even if you had a
gigabyte connection,

00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:04.530
it would still take four months.

00:02:04.530 --> 00:02:06.550
Now, who wants to
wait that long?

00:02:06.550 --> 00:02:08.560
The transfer appliance
on the other hand,

00:02:08.560 --> 00:02:12.360
can capture a petabyte
of data in just 25 hours.

00:02:12.360 --> 00:02:14.340
That's crazy fast.

00:02:14.340 --> 00:02:16.260
Now that our data's
in a Cloud, we're

00:02:16.260 --> 00:02:18.540
ready to run machine
learning training at scale.

00:02:18.540 --> 00:02:20.610
But that's a whole
topic of its own,

00:02:20.610 --> 00:02:23.460
which we will cover
in our next episode.

00:02:23.460 --> 00:02:25.920
Training machine-learning
models on large sets

00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:28.590
can be challenging to
accomplish with limited compute

00:02:28.590 --> 00:02:29.790
and storage resources.

00:02:29.790 --> 00:02:31.830
But it doesn't have
to be that way.

00:02:31.830 --> 00:02:35.160
By moving to the Cloud via
either gsutil or the transfer

00:02:35.160 --> 00:02:37.530
appliance, you can
train on large data sets

00:02:37.530 --> 00:02:39.320
without any hiccups.

00:02:39.320 --> 00:02:42.280
I'm Yufeng Guo, and thanks for
watching this episode of Cloud

00:02:42.280 --> 00:02:43.476
AI Adventures.

00:02:43.476 --> 00:02:45.100
On our next episode,
we'll be exploring

00:02:45.100 --> 00:02:47.475
the other side of running
machine learning in the Cloud--

00:02:47.475 --> 00:02:48.730
the compute side.

00:02:48.730 --> 00:02:51.880
We'll even be exploring the
options when it comes to GPUs.

00:02:51.880 --> 00:02:53.830
But for now, remember,
when you need

00:02:53.830 --> 00:02:55.870
to do machine-learning
on big data sets,

00:02:55.870 --> 00:02:57.986
put your data in the cloud.

00:02:57.986 --> 00:02:59.360
Thanks for watching
this episode.

